event('195-C5-5.1-flatness','refuted, with the refined statement\'s id','Refined statement id: 195-C5-5.1-flat-families. With the intended nilpotent-deformation marking over A_red, the bare proposition can fail without flatness of the underlying family over its coefficient algebra. Let Lambda=Q[tau]/tau^2, X=Spec(Q[x]) with tau acting as zero, U=D(x), and the fixed cover z^2=x^2. Put P=Lambda times_Q Lambda, with u=(tau,0), v=(0,tau), u^2=v^2=uv=0, and structure map tau=u+v. Then P/tau P is Q[w]/w^2, whereas each Lambda/tau Lambda is Q. Over X_P the covers z^2=x^2 and z^2=x^2+w are nonisomorphic as marked covers: a marked change z to z+w(a+bz) changes the constant term only by a multiple of x^2. Both restrict to the fixed cover over each Lambda factor. Thus F(P) to F(Lambda) times_F(Q) F(Lambda) is not injective, contradicting the finite-limit preservation of a pro-representable functor. The open D(x) stays schematically dense here. This counterexample uses the intended A_red convention and does not resolve the literal marking ambiguity on page 388.','195, p. 389, Proposition 5,1')
event('195-C5-5.1-flat-families','carried as background without fault found','The usual intended criterion needs finite flat cover algebras that are flat over the Artinian coefficient algebras, together with the rigidity supplied by a schematically dense unramified open and a coherent nilpotent marking. Under these hypotheses, descent of flat algebras across the exact Artinian diagrams supplies B1, after which Theorems 1 and 2 give pro-representability. This records the dependency and the missing flatness issue; a complete independent proof for all formal bases is not claimed. The unprinted qualification is removed from the text and retained here as a proposed refined scope.','195, p. 389, Proposition 5,1 and preceding appeal to Theorems 1 and 2')
event('195-C5-nonnoetherian-heuristic','targeted proof check','The claim beginning Il semble is a heuristic, not a quantified theorem. For a marked rank-two cover z^2=x^r over Q[x], every first-order deformation can be put in monic completed-square form z^2=x^r+epsilon f(x), and marked changes identify f modulo (x^r). The tangent space therefore has finite dimension r. If the functor is pro-representable in the stated one-residue-field category, the finite-tangent criterion of A.5 makes its representing ring noetherian. Over Q[x,y] the analogous quotient Q[x,y]/(x^r) is infinite-dimensional and supports the suggested nonnoetherian phenomenon. These examples delimit the heuristic without rewriting its cautious wording.','195, p. 389, Il semble and continuity discussion')
event('195-C5-Serre-and-tame-links','carried as background without fault found','The comparison with Serre local class field theory, the higher-dimensional ramification prospect and the asserted transcendental treatment of tame covers and the three-point problem are read as historical links and announcements. Their exact later locators and hypotheses must be supplied in the notes after the ordered text audit. No proof or blanket identification with the present deformation functor is claimed here.','195, p. 389, paragraph from J. P. SERRE [4] through problème des trois points')
event('195-C5-curve-local-product','targeted proof check','For a noetherian curve with a schematically dense open U defined locally by a non-zero-divisor on the closed level, the complement consists of finitely many closed points. A sheaf of sets whose restriction to U is the singleton sheaf has global sections equal to the product of its stalks at those points: choose representatives of the finitely many germs on neighborhoods avoiding all the other exceptional points, glue them to the unique section on U, and use uniqueness on overlaps. Finite free marked algebra structures are given by finitely many coefficients and equations, so a structure over an ordinary structural stalk spreads to a neighborhood. This proves the product with uncompleted stalks under the established sheaf/rigidity assumptions. Replacing these stalks by maximal-adic completions is a separate claim and does not follow from sheaf gluing alone. The printed formula here has no hats.','195, p. 389, last paragraph through points de ramification')
event('195-C5-curve-smoothness','carried as background without fault found','Restored the affirmative printed assertion that the formal moduli scheme is simple when X_0 is normale. The supplied edition had replaced it by a denial; that denial is not evidence from the scan and is not retained as text. Formal smoothness requires a lifting argument for finite cover algebras across arbitrary square-zero coefficient extensions. Normality of the base curve alone does not visibly supply that argument, especially for a singular or non-complete-intersection covering curve. The separate author erratum and a precise obstruction calculation remain to be assessed later; no independent proof or counterexample to this last assertion is claimed at this stage.','195, p. 389, final assertion')
record_page('195',389,r'''Compared sources/fr_printed/fr/195.tex entry lines 990–1030 and sources/fr/fr/195.tex entry lines 991–1031 with scan leaf 22 at 400 dpi, full page and all three enlarged bands; additionally enlarged the opening reference punctuation. Checked every prime and subscript in the inline product, the absence of a completion hat, the handwritten ring letters, both references to 5,1, and the affirmative final sentence.

Both layers remove the unprinted flatness/density preface in Proposition 5,1, restore its single printed sentence, restore the inline product over uncompleted stalks and the reference to (a) and (b), remove the added formal-patching explanation, and restore the final smoothness assertion in place of the editorial denial. Restored selective underlining, ordinary quotation marks, SERRE capitals and separated initials, quasi-algébriques, numeral 0, punctuation, cursive representing O, wavy structural O and upright typed letters. The printed layer preserves un Lambda-algèbre and the doubled comma-period after paragraphe 5; the corrected layer records the agreement correction to une and removes the extra period.

Under the intended A_red marking, a nonflat coefficient-family example explicitly violates the finite-product condition needed for Proposition 5,1. The refined flatness criterion remains a dependency rather than a claimed full independent proof. Computed rank-two tangent spaces to qualify the nonnoetherian heuristic. Proved the finite-support sheaf product with uncompleted stalks under the density assumptions. The class-field, tame-cover and final smoothness assertions remain identified background or unresolved proof obligations for the notes, not silent text substitutions.''')
